# Comparing demosaic methods on the same sensor data.
#
# mosaic("test") is the undemosaiced sensor readout -- one sample per pixel,
# linear, at the sensor's full bit depth. image("test") would instead give the
# demosaiced result using the app's default method, which is what every other
# script gets without having to ask.
#
# Drop a raw file (.CR3, .CR2, .ARW, .NEF, ...) onto the "test" row.
# On a PNG or JPEG this script errors, deliberately: there is no mosaic to show.

raw = mosaic("test")

a = choose("method A", "demosaic", demosaic_consistent)
b = choose("method B", "demosaic", demosaic_ahd)

outA = params(a, "A")(raw)
outB = params(b, "B")(raw)

display(outA, "1 method A")
display(outB, "2 method B")

# Pick demosaic_passthrough in either dropdown to see the raw Bayer pattern
# itself -- zoom in and the colour filter array is visible, one colour per
# pixel, before any interpolation.
#
# The four methods, in the order they were added:
#
# demosaic_bilinear averages the neighbours. A correct LOW-PASS reconstruction
# -- downsample its output and its error largely vanishes -- but it discards
# high-frequency detail.
#
# demosaic_malvar adds a gradient correction from the centre channel. Better,
# and still a fixed kernel: the same weights everywhere, no per-pixel decisions.
#
# demosaic_ahd is adaptive. It interpolates green ALONG edges rather than across
# them, so thin high-contrast detail -- bare branches against sky, wires, hair
# -- keeps its colour instead of breaking into coloured speckles. Against a
# known scene it scores 36.1 dB where Malvar scores 26.4.
#
# demosaic_consistent takes the opposite approach and is the current default.
# Rather than interpolating more cleverly, it starts from bilinear and RESTORES
# the detail bilinear discarded, using the sensor's own samples: at each site
# the sensor measured one channel exactly, so a reconstruction can be checked
# against it. The correction is applied to luminance only, leaving the colour
# differences alone, so it cannot introduce false colour.
#
# ITS DETAIL ADVANTAGE IS REAL; ITS COLOUR ADVANTAGE IS NOT. Re-measured on
# _L0A0738.CR2 after the camera matrix stopped manufacturing negatives:
#
#                      false magenta   chroma SD   detail
#   bilinear                  16876      0.4874    0.0054  (100%)
#   malvar                     9282      0.4876    0.0067  (124%)
#   ahd                        9790      0.4864    0.0065  (120%)
#   consistent                17415      0.4870    0.0084  (156%)
#
# Detail holds up -- 156% of bilinear against AHD's 120%. But on false magenta
# (a pixel developing magenta although its green photosites were the brightest
# of the three) consistent is the WORST of the four, marginally behind plain
# bilinear, while Malvar and AHD are near half that.
#
# Sweeping its own controls shows the correction is not merely failing to help,
# it is actively costing:
#
#   strength 0,   median 0    16876   (this IS bilinear)
#   strength 0.5, median 0    15945
#   strength 1,   median 0    17388
#   strength 1,   median 1    17415
#   strength 1,   median 2    17771
#
# The chroma median exists to suppress false colour and makes it worse. The
# earlier claim of "72% chroma noise against AHD's 74%" does not reproduce --
# all four now sit within 0.2% of each other on that measure, so it was not
# separating the methods in the first place.
#
# The honest reading: the correction restores luminance detail well, and its
# apparent colour advantage was measured while a separate bug -- the camera
# matrix driving channels negative around highlights -- dominated the numbers.
# With that fixed, the colour claim does not survive. What it does to false
# colour near highlights is an open problem, not a solved one.
#
# Its `strength` reduces it exactly to bilinear at 0, which is worth trying:
# sweeping it from 0 to 1 shows precisely what the correction is doing.
